# Growing Random Graphs with Quantum Rules

Python implementation, comparison with classical growth, and a reproduction of the
results of **Jnane, H.; Di Molfetta, G.; Miatto, F. M. (2020). "Growing Random Graphs
with Quantum Rules." EPTCS 315, pp. 38–47.** DOI: [10.4204/EPTCS.315.4](https://doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.315.4)

> **What this project is about.** A node is added to a graph, one at a time. Instead
> of the classical "richer gets richer" rule (preferential attachment), the target
> node(s) are chosen by letting a **quantum walker** evolve on the current graph for a
> random time and then measuring its position (the Born rule). Because the walker's
> amplitudes can interfere, the resulting graphs are structurally different from both
> classical random graphs and preferential-attachment graphs. Everything here is a
> **classical simulation** of that quantum process — no quantum hardware is involved.

For the full write-up — theoretical background, a step-by-step account of how every
figure is built, the numbers behind each claim, limitations, and a detailed
bibliography — see
[`docs/growing_random_graphs_quantum_rules_documentation.docx`](docs/growing_random_graphs_quantum_rules_documentation.docx).
This README is a shorter, practical companion to that document.

---

## Project structure

```
.
├── README.md
├── main.ipynb                          # orchestrates all 4 stages, displays the figures inline
├── docs/
│   └── growing_random_graphs_quantum_rules_documentation.docx
├── src/
│   ├── grafo_quantico.py               # Stage 1: simple quantum-inspired model
│   ├── grafo_quantico_passo_a_passo.py # Stage 2: growth phases (static GIFs)
│   ├── grafo_classico_passo_a_passo.py # Stage 3: classical growth (comparison)
│   └── reproduce_jnane_paper.py        # Stage 4: paper reproduction (Figs. 2–8 + extra)
│
│   # created on first run, at the working directory the scripts are launched from:
├── fases/                              # Stage 2 output: static GIFs (quantum)
├── fases_classico/                     # Stage 3 output: static GIFs (classical)
└── paper_figures/                      # Stage 4 output: Figs. 2–8, extra check, results.pkl
```

> **Naming note.** Stages 1–3 keep their original Portuguese file names and internal
> identifiers (`grafo_quantico.py`, `crescer_com_historico`, `graus`, …); Stage 4 was
> translated to English (`reproduce_jnane_paper.py`, `grow_graph`, `degrees`, …). Both
> are functionally complete and independent of each other.

## Requirements

Python 3.9+ (tested on 3.12). No GPU or quantum hardware needed.

```bash
pip install numpy scipy networkx matplotlib pillow
```

| Package | Used for |
|---|---|
| `numpy` | linear algebra (diagonalization, matrix exponential), random sampling |
| `scipy` | sparse matrix exponential (`expm_multiply`), shortest paths (`csgraph`) — Stage 4 only |
| `networkx` | graph data structure, layout, drawing |
| `matplotlib` | all figures |
| `pillow` | PNG → GIF conversion (matplotlib cannot write GIF files directly) |

## Quick start

```bash
git clone <this-repo>
cd <this-repo>
pip install numpy scipy networkx matplotlib pillow
jupyter notebook main.ipynb
```

`main.ipynb` runs all four stages end to end and displays every figure inline. Each
stage can also be run stand-alone from a terminal (commands below); run them from the
project root so the output folders land in the right place.

## The four stages

### Stage 1 — Simple quantum-inspired model

```bash
python src/grafo_quantico.py
```

A minimal model, not a strict reproduction of the paper: a quantum walker evolves for a
**fixed** time `t = exp(-iAt)` and the new node attaches to `m` nodes sampled from the
resulting Born-rule probabilities. Cost is `O(n³)` per step (`O(N⁴)` for the whole run),
so it is only meant for hundreds of nodes. Produces `grafo_quantico.png` and prints
basic statistics.

### Stage 2 — Growth phases (static GIFs)

```bash
python src/grafo_quantico_passo_a_passo.py
```

Re-runs the Stage 1 process but stores the full growth history and renders selected
phases (steps 0, 5, 10, 20, 30, 37 by default) as single-frame GIFs. Node color encodes
the Born probability used to sample it; the new node is red. Output: `fases/fase_XX.gif`.

### Stage 3 — Classical growth (comparison)

```bash
python src/grafo_classico_passo_a_passo.py
```

Same visualization as Stage 2, but the new node is chosen by a classical rule instead
of a quantum walk:

- `REGRA = "barabasi"` (default) — preferential attachment, `p ∝ degree`.
- `REGRA = "passeio_classico"` — classical continuous-time random walk, `p(t) = e^{-Lt} p(0)`.

Also prints a quick quantum-vs-classical comparison table. Output:
`fases_classico/fase_XX.gif`.

### Stage 4 — Reproducing the paper

```bash
python src/reproduce_jnane_paper.py [--fast] [--samples N] [--reuse]
```

Implements the paper's 1-, 2- and 3-walker models with an exponentially distributed
collapse time `t ~ Exp(τ)`, and reproduces Figures 2–8 of the paper plus an extra check
of the `E[n] ≈ 1/τ` mean star-size estimate. In Jupyter, call the equivalent function
directly instead of using the CLI flags:

```python
import sys; sys.path.insert(0, "src")
import reproduce_jnane_paper as paper

paper.main(fast=True)     # lightweight run (a couple of minutes)
paper.main()               # full run — ≈7 min on one core, matches the documentation exactly
paper.main(reuse=True)     # only rebuild the figures from a previous run's paper_figures/results.pkl
```

Output: `paper_figures/fig2_trees_1_walker.png` … `fig8_clustering.png`,
`extra_star_size.png`, and `paper_figures/results.pkl` (cached simulation results).

## Results at a glance

Qualitative agreement with the paper's claims (full numbers and discussion in
`docs/…docx`, Section 5):

| Claim in the paper | Status |
|---|---|
| Small τ → nodes form large stars | Reproduced |
| Mean star size `E[n] ≈ 1/τ` | Reproduced (factor ≈ 0.9) |
| Eigenvalues ≈ √m per star | Reproduced (qualitative) |
| Degree distribution → power law for large τ | Partial (curve shape matches; no formal statistical fit) |
| Diameter maximal for `0.1 < τ < 1` | Reproduced |
| Leaf fraction stabilizes at τ ≈ 1, size-independent | Reproduced |
| Clustering is 0 for 1 walker, decreases with τ | Reproduced (except at τ = 0.001) |
| Possible phase transition at τ ≈ 1 | Not tested |

## Known limitations

- This is a **classical simulation** of a quantum dynamics: worst-case `O(n³)` cost per
  growth step, which limits graph size (hundreds to a few thousand nodes).
- Stage 4's statistical figures use only 8 samples per point over 500–600-node graphs;
  shaded bands are ±1 standard deviation, not confidence intervals.
- Some details are not specified in the paper (initial position of multiple walkers,
  meaning of the shaded bands in its own figures) and were filled in with a reasonable
  interpretation — see Section 3.4.2 of the documentation.
- The quantum-vs-classical comparison (Stage 3) is illustrative, not from the paper, and
  uses a single random seed.

